Actually, it seems that other hash implementations seems to rely on the use of the chain style should an insertion happen with an already used bucket and the performance of insertion and retrival is fine.

So... what if I ask a different question on the matter. What if I used a sparse approach? Allocate only what I need when I need it.

Mind you that there will be an allocated space of memory to speed up it's allocations. But buckets are only created when they are needed. They are allocated to a dynamically sizing pool array, and will get reused.

    class SpatialHashBucket {
        private:
        int ID;
        [ORGANIZATION];
        int *Grid_ID_Index; //Populated after the insertion phase, stores an index to other grids.
        int *buffer
        public:
        ...
    }

And each bucket simply stores a few extra bits of data. The implementation is BigO(N) all around, but I have been proven that being incredibly fast still, and still faster than bouncing around on too many pointers. With all allocations happening on a buffer of pre-slotted memory the framerates won't dip too much.

I can always try to use another map library... but I don't know any beyond [ORGANIZATION] that allows for multiple items to have the same collision. Nor do I know any that allows a custom hash function.

How does that simply shader code exactly? O.o;

It seems pretty simple to me the way it is.

But thanks, I didn't think about using a really large buffer. I take it you mark it with the STREAM flag?
